00:03:00.939 --> 00:03:03.639
a spec and rough when it comes to having them fire

73
00:03:03.639 --> 00:03:05.860
and be on when it comes to sequencer.

74
00:03:05.860 --> 00:03:06.780
Let me show you what I mean.

75
00:03:06.780 --> 00:03:09.169
We'll pick on these guys first.

76
00:03:09.169 --> 00:03:12.449
So these two fellows work very differently from each other.

77
00:03:12.449 --> 00:03:13.330
And what I mean by that,

78
00:03:13.330 --> 00:03:16.990
let me go ahead and disconnect my roughness.

79
00:03:16.990 --> 00:03:19.389
Now you'll notice immediately when I disconnect my roughness,

80
00:03:19.389 --> 00:03:20.810
it's not quite as shiny.

81
00:03:20.810 --> 00:03:22.930
We see that they're complementing each other.

82
00:03:22.930 --> 00:03:27.340
But we'll notice that if I disconnect my spec

83
00:03:27.340 --> 00:03:29.259
and reconnect,

84
00:03:29.259 --> 00:03:30.740
and you'll notice there's still a little bit of spec

85
00:03:30.740 --> 00:03:35.740
going on because default spec is 0.5, it's kind of halfway.

86
00:03:36.620 --> 00:03:39.460
If I reconnect, put my rough in here,

87
00:03:39.460 --> 00:03:41.740
you'll notice, hey, we got shininess.

88
00:03:41.740 --> 00:03:43.539
And then if you reconnect our spec,

89
00:03:43.539 --> 00:03:46.020
you'll notice, oh, it's a little bit more shiny.

90
00:03:46.020 --> 00:03:47.219
They're complementing each other,

91
00:03:47.219 --> 00:03:49.659
but there's a difference between on and off.

92
00:03:49.659 --> 00:03:52.979
When it comes to rough, zero is on.

93
00:03:52.979 --> 00:03:55.699
When it comes to spec, one is on.

94
00:03:55.699 --> 00:03:57.180
I know it's a bit confusing,

95
00:03:57.180 --> 00:04:00.979
but the problem is if we start to animate this in Sequencer,

96
00:04:00.979 --> 00:04:02.580
we could actually get our numbers

97
00:04:02.580 --> 00:04:05.240
and not quite get the values we exactly want.

98
00:04:05.240 --> 00:04:07.219
You can actually play with these in such a way

99
00:04:07.219 --> 00:04:11.500
and have them initially set to be on within your MPC

100
00:04:11.500 --> 00:04:12.500
if you wanted to.

101
00:04:12.500 --> 00:04:15.340
So in our spec, we want to actually reverse these.

102
00:04:15.340 --> 00:04:20.720
We want spec to be zero and we want rough.

103
00:04:20.720 --> 00:04:22.339
And you'll notice when I hit zero,

104
00:04:22.339 --> 00:04:23.560
rough is automatically turned off.

105
00:04:23.560 --> 00:04:24.759
That's not fair.

106
00:04:24.759 --> 00:04:28.199
So that's because rough spec will influence rough

107
00:04:28.199 --> 00:04:29.519
and mess things up.

108
00:04:29.519 --> 00:04:32.279
We want rough to be also off.

109
00:04:32.279 --> 00:04:35.579
And when he's off, he's set to one.

110
00:04:35.579 --> 00:04:38.360
So we want to set these guys both in that,

111
00:04:38.360 --> 00:04:40.680
sitting with that and those numbers.

112
00:04:40.680 --> 00:04:43.040
So let's go to spec and set that to zero.

113
00:04:43.040 --> 00:04:44.160
It's already there.

114
00:04:44.160 --> 00:04:48.589
And we can set our rough already to one.

115
00:04:48.589 --> 00:04:50.470
Now, if you want to, you can make this negative.

116
00:04:50.470 --> 00:04:52.810
Now these are just scalar parameters.

117
00:04:52.810 --> 00:04:54.350
And I did a test on this

118
00:04:54.389 --> 00:04:57.149
and this actually worked okay to keep this at one

119
00:04:57.149 --> 00:04:57.990
if we wanted to.

120
00:04:57.990 --> 00:04:59.829
You can try keeping it at zero,

121
00:04:59.829 --> 00:05:02.149
but you'll have to play with those numbers in sequencer

122
00:05:02.149 --> 00:05:04.829
because remember it acts differently.

123
00:05:04.829 --> 00:05:09.959
One is on and zero is off

124
00:05:12.439 --> 00:05:16.500
in the case of when you're looking at spec.

125
00:05:16.500 --> 00:05:19.220
So spec one is on, zero is off

126
00:05:19.220 --> 00:05:21.019
and rough it's opposite of that.

127
00:05:21.019 --> 00:05:22.620
One is off and zero is off.

128
00:05:22.620 --> 00:05:24.579
So that's where it can get really confusing.

129
00:05:25.339 --> 00:05:28.420
I'm putting myself in a place so that they're neutral.

130
00:05:28.420 --> 00:05:30.339
And again, they can influence each other.

131
00:05:30.339 --> 00:05:32.860
I should say at a starting point

132
00:05:32.860 --> 00:05:34.420
where they're just not on

133
00:05:34.420 --> 00:05:36.100
and they can influence each other.

134
00:05:36.100 --> 00:05:39.910
So if I go to spec and turn this back on again

135
00:05:39.910 --> 00:05:45.170
and then I turn rough back on again,

136
00:05:45.170 --> 00:05:48.779
you can now see just how bright and shiny they are.

137
00:05:48.779 --> 00:05:52.879
Then if we go to specular and turn it off

138
00:05:52.879 --> 00:05:55.560
and notice that mutes are rough, that's not fair.

139
00:05:55.600 --> 00:05:57.759
We'll have to completely disconnect it

140
00:05:57.759 --> 00:05:59.139
to be able to get rough to come back.

141
00:05:59.139 --> 00:06:01.600
So this is what I mean by you gotta be careful.

142
00:06:01.600 --> 00:06:03.879
These guys are gonna push and pull on each other.

143
00:06:03.879 --> 00:06:05.480
Not necessarily the most ideal,

144
00:06:05.480 --> 00:06:07.959
but when you're animating in sequencer,

145
00:06:07.959 --> 00:06:09.839
you have to keep those things in mind.

146
00:06:09.839 --> 00:06:11.199
All right, let me disconnect these completely.

147
00:06:11.199 --> 00:06:12.879
You actually just delete them.

148
00:06:12.879 --> 00:06:13.839
Now, when it comes to color,

